WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.201
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:02.201 --> 00:00:04.937
is an Emmy-winning writer,
actor, and comedian.

00:00:04.937 --> 00:00:07.707
Her musical "The Bedwetter"
makes its off-Broadway debut

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:09.775
on May 23rd at
the Atlantic Theater Company.

00:00:09.775 --> 00:00:10.877
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:10.877 --> 00:00:12.879
our friend Sarah Silverman,
everybody!

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:14.847
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.847 --> 00:00:21.020 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:27.193 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:30.429
How are you?
-Oh, I'm very good.

00:00:30.429 --> 00:00:35.167
I just bumped into
Jeffrey Donovan backstage.

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:37.569
-Yeah, our other guest tonight.

00:00:37.569 --> 00:00:39.272
-Very exciting.
-You're a fan.

00:00:39.272 --> 00:00:42.474
-I'm a big "Law &amp; Order" fan,
my friend -- "L&amp;O."

00:00:42.474 --> 00:00:44.210
-I'm very happy
about this new --

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:45.879
This new "Law &amp; Order"
is wonderful.

00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:47.613
-I can't believe it, and he's --

00:00:47.613 --> 00:00:50.150
You know, Anthony Anderson,
he's back as Bernard.

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:51.217
-Yep.

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:54.987
-Then Jeffrey is Frank Cosgrove.

00:00:54.987 --> 00:00:57.189
-Mm-hmm.
-And he's got great lines.

00:00:57.189 --> 00:00:58.691
I watch it every Thursday.

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:01.227
Like, this last one,
he had so many good lines.

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:02.728
Like, he goes --

00:01:02.728 --> 00:01:06.099
Anthony Anderson character
Bernard, he goes,

00:01:06.099 --> 00:01:08.067
"Hey, nice move out there."

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:11.971
And then Frank goes, "Hey, I
don't sell shoes for a living."

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:14.140
[ Laughter ]

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:17.911
And then I even Googled that,
'cause I go, "Is that a saying?"

00:01:17.911 --> 00:01:19.412
But it's not.
-Yeah.

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:20.847
It's a very unique role

00:01:20.847 --> 00:01:23.749
that they always need somebody
on "Law &amp; Order" to perform.

00:01:23.749 --> 00:01:25.351
It's called the Orbach.
You need the Orbach.

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:28.154
-Yes, that last --
right before the "bow bow."

00:01:28.154 --> 00:01:30.657
-Yeah, you've got to
have the guy who's like,

00:01:30.657 --> 00:01:32.225
"I'm gonna say
something good enough

00:01:32.225 --> 00:01:33.860
that you can 'bow bow' me."

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:35.261
-Then they catch the crook,

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:37.964
and the crook's a real jerk,
you know, and the guy --

00:01:37.964 --> 00:01:40.233
and he's putting him
in the cop car,

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:42.468
and the guy goes,
"I want a lawyer."

00:01:42.468 --> 00:01:44.870
And Cosgrove goes,
"You're gonna need a priest."

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:45.872
-Oh!

00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:47.106
♪ Bow bow ♪

00:01:47.106 --> 00:01:49.241
[ Laughter ]

00:01:49.241 --> 00:01:50.910
-Anyway...

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.645
-Enough about our second guest.

00:01:52.645 --> 00:01:54.847
-You're right.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:54.847 --> 00:01:56.115
-This is very exciting.

00:01:56.115 --> 00:01:57.584
This is based on your memoir

00:01:57.584 --> 00:01:59.785
about you being
a 10-year-old bedwetter.

00:01:59.785 --> 00:02:00.954
True story.
-Yeah.

00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:02.421
-And so now it's a musical,

00:02:02.421 --> 00:02:03.823
and there is a young actor

00:02:03.823 --> 00:02:05.158
playing a 10-year-old
version of you.

00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:06.526
Is that weird to watch?

00:02:06.526 --> 00:02:08.594
I mean, I'm sure the whole
process is a little surreal.

00:02:08.594 --> 00:02:11.664
-I mean, sure, it's weird,
but we've been working on it.

00:02:11.664 --> 00:02:14.867
I've been working on it so long,
10 years, you know,

00:02:14.867 --> 00:02:16.602
that it's like, "Wow, she was,

00:02:16.602 --> 00:02:19.271
you know, like, little
when we started work."

00:02:19.271 --> 00:02:22.174
But this actress is --
Like, she's incredible.

00:02:22.174 --> 00:02:23.509
It's crazy to watch.

00:02:23.509 --> 00:02:27.147
It is surreal, but now I'm
very used to it, you know.

00:02:27.147 --> 00:02:29.115
But she's just --
Her name is Zoe Glick,

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:32.785
and she just carries this
whole show on her shoulders.

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:33.953
She's incredible.

00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:35.788
-It's very exciting that
you're opening on Monday.

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:37.223
It's very exciting.

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:39.191
As you said, this is the end
of a long journey of 10 years.

00:02:39.191 --> 00:02:41.160
Where did you rehearse
in the city?

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.662
-We rehearsed at a rehearsal
hall in Times Square.

00:02:43.662 --> 00:02:44.831
-That's very New York.

00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:46.932
-So I'm walking her
through Times Square.

00:02:46.932 --> 00:02:49.936
And it's so funny because, you
know, Carolines is right there.

00:02:49.936 --> 00:02:52.171
-Very famous comedy club, yep.
-Right.

00:02:52.171 --> 00:02:56.809
And a kid is passing out flyers
for Carolines.

00:02:56.809 --> 00:02:58.577
"Hey, live comedy, live comedy."

00:02:58.577 --> 00:03:01.246
And I'm like, "I'm gonna
make this kid's day

00:03:01.246 --> 00:03:02.915
'cause he must be
a young comic."

00:03:02.915 --> 00:03:05.417
I passed out flyers for
two years when I started out.

00:03:05.417 --> 00:03:09.722
So I walk over to him, and I go,
"No, thank you."

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:13.026
And he goes, "Okay."

00:03:13.026 --> 00:03:14.994
[ Laughter ]

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:19.898
So...
-That's a rough moment.

00:03:19.898 --> 00:03:22.268
-Put me in my place.
It's probably good for me.

00:03:22.268 --> 00:03:23.936
It was good for me.
-So, you get to sit --

00:03:23.936 --> 00:03:25.404
You know, again,
these are previews,

00:03:25.404 --> 00:03:26.806
so people,
they're doing the play.

00:03:26.806 --> 00:03:27.940
You're getting to watch it.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:29.575
You're getting to watch it
with an audience,

00:03:29.575 --> 00:03:31.444
taking notes in the back.
-Yeah.

00:03:31.444 --> 00:03:32.778
-And what is that like?

00:03:32.778 --> 00:03:35.047
Do you feel like you're getting
a lot out of your notes?

00:03:35.047 --> 00:03:37.784
-I mean, real --
Yeah, like, just --

00:03:37.784 --> 00:03:40.986
You know, I collaborated
with this incredible writer,

00:03:40.986 --> 00:03:44.123
Josh Harmon, and the incredible
David Yazbek.

00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:46.091
And we sit in the back there,

00:03:46.091 --> 00:03:48.794
and we've got our legal pads
or whatever,

00:03:48.794 --> 00:03:50.796
and I'm scribbling notes.

00:03:50.796 --> 00:03:54.700
And then when I go home, I look
at them and I type them up.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:56.903
And I was looking at them
one night,

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:59.205
and I don't know how to explain.

00:03:59.205 --> 00:04:00.606
I've never said this out loud.

00:04:00.606 --> 00:04:05.277
Like, the character
who plays my sister Laura

00:04:05.277 --> 00:04:09.448
is talking to little 10-year-old
Sarah, and she says --

00:04:09.448 --> 00:04:12.485
she's telling her what she was
like when she was a baby

00:04:12.485 --> 00:04:14.753
and "How I used to
change your diaper."

00:04:14.753 --> 00:04:17.056
And then she says,
"And sometimes you would fart."

00:04:17.056 --> 00:04:20.326
And she would kind of swallow
the end of the sentence.

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:21.927
And so I was looking
at my notes,

00:04:21.927 --> 00:04:25.397
and it says, "Laura,
don't swallow fart."

00:04:25.397 --> 00:04:28.634
And, yeah,
I took a picture of it.

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:31.403
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:31.403 --> 00:04:34.240
-When I was -- You know,
you're so serious back there.

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:36.742
And then as I'm typing it up, I
just laughed so hard because --

00:04:36.742 --> 00:04:40.513
not just at the sentence,
"Laura, don't swallow fart,"

00:04:40.513 --> 00:04:43.449
but just how serious
I know I was when I wrote it.

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:44.450
-Yeah.
-I was like...

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:47.219
[ Laughter ]

00:04:47.219 --> 00:04:51.256
So, the true story is,
you went to see a hypnotist

00:04:51.256 --> 00:04:53.892
when you were 10 years old

00:04:53.892 --> 00:04:56.062
as a means of dealing
with this problem.

00:04:56.062 --> 00:04:59.532
And was it still
fresh in your head?

00:04:59.532 --> 00:05:01.166
Like, do you have
vivid memories of it?

00:05:01.166 --> 00:05:04.403
-You know, I don't remember
yesterday or last week

00:05:04.403 --> 00:05:09.909
or like four hours ago,
but I remember it so vividly.

00:05:09.909 --> 00:05:12.745
And it's funny 'cause it's
a great song in the show --

00:05:12.745 --> 00:05:15.915
this hypnotist, Dr. Grimm,
which I didn't change his name.

00:05:15.915 --> 00:05:19.452
I don't know, I guess, so far,
it's not been a problem.

00:05:19.452 --> 00:05:21.320
And then there's little Sarah.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:23.890
And I remember this hypnotist,

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:27.893
and I remember him
having me close my eyes

00:05:27.893 --> 00:05:29.194
and talking me through.

00:05:29.194 --> 00:05:31.263
And there's a stream,
and you walk to a clearing.

00:05:31.263 --> 00:05:32.731
And I'm just like,
"What's a clearing?"

00:05:32.731 --> 00:05:35.267
I'm 10. Like, I couldn't
figure out what it was.

00:05:35.267 --> 00:05:37.436
And then I remember my whole --

00:05:37.436 --> 00:05:40.139
desperately trying
to be hypnotized,

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:43.542
but my brain was just like,
"This chair is blue.

00:05:43.542 --> 00:05:45.778
He has a penis and balls."

00:05:45.778 --> 00:05:48.814
You know, and then I was like,
"How does he wipe?

00:05:48.814 --> 00:05:51.584
Do boys wipe front to back
like girls?"

00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:54.287
And then, like, "Where does
his front even start?"

00:05:54.287 --> 00:05:56.789
And it's this whole
inner monologue,

00:05:56.789 --> 00:06:00.059
and it's in the song
and it's so funny.

00:06:00.059 --> 00:06:02.328
Every time I watch it,
you know, I still --

00:06:02.328 --> 00:06:03.963
That's one thing where I'm like,

00:06:03.963 --> 00:06:06.799
"This is, like,
literally my thought."

00:06:06.799 --> 00:06:08.333
Like, exactly what was
going through --

00:06:08.333 --> 00:06:09.802
-It's authentic
to a 10-year-old.

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:12.004
-Yeah, it was my entire
inner monologue.

00:06:12.004 --> 00:06:13.940
-You knew stand-up was something
you wanted to do early on.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:17.042
Do you remember early jokes?

00:06:17.042 --> 00:06:21.513
-Oh, yeah, I was --
I had really terrible --

00:06:21.513 --> 00:06:23.515
I don't remember
doing this joke,

00:06:23.515 --> 00:06:25.617
but I was looking
at old notebooks.

00:06:25.617 --> 00:06:27.052
I don't save things.

00:06:27.052 --> 00:06:30.656
I'm not very sentimental,
but I have all my notebooks

00:06:30.656 --> 00:06:33.358
since I was like 19,
maybe, or 18.

00:06:33.358 --> 00:06:36.595
And there's a joke in there
that's so bad, it made me laugh,

00:06:36.595 --> 00:06:37.963
just that it's terrible.

00:06:37.963 --> 00:06:42.234
It said, "I don't know
why my goldfish died.

00:06:42.234 --> 00:06:47.406
I put it in a tank --
dot, dot, dot -- top."

00:06:47.406 --> 00:06:49.842
[ Laughter ]

00:06:49.842 --> 00:06:52.678
Just seeing, like --
seeing how I wrote it

00:06:52.678 --> 00:06:55.214
how it's to be performed.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:55.214 --> 00:06:57.583
-It won't work if you just say,
"I put it in the tank top,"

00:06:57.583 --> 00:06:59.718
but if you put those two --
you put those dots in there.

00:06:59.718 --> 00:07:02.922
-Yeah, "top."

00:07:02.922 --> 00:07:05.691
-I'm always very happy
to talk about your father.

00:07:05.691 --> 00:07:07.426
A very funny man
all these years.

00:07:07.426 --> 00:07:08.560
A man who loves comedy.

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.362
Now, you know,
there's a character

00:07:10.362 --> 00:07:11.764
in the show who's him, right?

00:07:11.764 --> 00:07:15.067
And has he come and seen
the show, and does he like it?

00:07:15.067 --> 00:07:19.605
-He came Friday night,
Saturday matinee,

00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:22.675
Saturday night, Sunday matinee,
and Sunday night.

00:07:22.675 --> 00:07:25.111
Five shows in three days.
-That is a dad!

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:27.980
You got a dad on your hands!

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:30.883
-Oh, my God.
-A-list dadding, right there.

00:07:30.883 --> 00:07:33.386
-I know. I mean,
he's been saying for years,

00:07:33.386 --> 00:07:36.689
"How much longer do I have to
stay alive for this thing?"

00:07:36.689 --> 00:07:39.525
And now it's like, "Oh,
I'm so glad he's seen it."

00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:42.061
But he said it was
the best weekend of his life.

00:07:42.061 --> 00:07:43.462
-That's amazing.

00:07:43.462 --> 00:07:45.498
Also amazing, and thank you
for sharing this,

00:07:45.498 --> 00:07:46.865
these are the texts he sent.

00:07:46.865 --> 00:07:49.668
This is April Fools' Day.
-His favorite day.

00:07:49.668 --> 00:07:51.404
-And he tried to tell you guys

00:07:51.404 --> 00:07:54.040
that your stepmother knocked out
his teeth, right?

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:56.108
-Yeah, so, very upsetting.
"Janice got --"

00:07:56.108 --> 00:07:58.444
I can't read well 'cause
I don't have my glasses.

00:07:58.444 --> 00:08:01.279
-Yeah, "Janice got pissed at me
and knocked my teeth out.

00:08:01.279 --> 00:08:02.515
I'm going to sue her."

00:08:02.515 --> 00:08:04.116
And then, "Pic of me
without my teeth."

00:08:04.116 --> 00:08:07.152
-That's my favorite is that he
didn't just send the picture.

00:08:07.152 --> 00:08:10.056
He wrote "Pic of me
without my teeth."

00:08:10.056 --> 00:08:12.024
[ Laughter ]

00:08:12.024 --> 00:08:13.192
-And then down here,
it just said,

00:08:13.192 --> 00:08:14.994
"I can only eat soup
and ice cream. Very sad."

00:08:14.994 --> 00:08:17.263
And then, "Eleeza," "Eliza?"
-Eleeza.

00:08:17.263 --> 00:08:19.264
-"Eleeza, you have spaghetti
on your head."

00:08:19.264 --> 00:08:20.933
What does that mean?

00:08:20.933 --> 00:08:25.637
-That was, like, an April Fools
that he had just with Eleeza.

00:08:25.637 --> 00:08:27.206
That was just a sidebar.

00:08:27.206 --> 00:08:29.040
-And so his April Fools joke
is to tell --

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:30.509
-She probably was like
[Gasps]

00:08:30.509 --> 00:08:31.910
[ Laughter ]

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:35.314
She's 29, by the way.
She's 29.

00:08:35.314 --> 00:08:36.682
-Sarah Silverman, everybody.

00:08:36.682 --> 00:08:40.119
"The Bedwetter" makes
its off-Broadway debut

00:08:40.119 --> 00:08:42.622
on May 23rd at
the Atlantic Theater Company.

00:08:42.622 --> 00:08:44.390
We'll be right back
with Jeffrey Donovan.

